.Dd June 3, 2026
.Dt XTC_OSPROC 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_osproc_spawn ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_isolated_spawn ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_pid ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_ctrl_fd ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_signal ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_call ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_serve ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_try_wait ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_wait ,
.Nm xtc_osproc_destroy
.Nd OS-process spawn, control socket, and lifecycle
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_osproc.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_osproc_spawn "const xtc_osproc_opts_t *opts" "xtc_osproc_t **out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_osproc_isolated_spawn "const char *name" "int (*fn)(int ctrl_fd, void *arg)" "void *arg" "xtc_osproc_t **out"
.Ft long
.Fn xtc_osproc_pid "const xtc_osproc_t *p"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_osproc_ctrl_fd "const xtc_osproc_t *p"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_osproc_signal "const xtc_osproc_t *p" "int sig"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_osproc_call "xtc_osproc_t *p" "const void *req" "size_t req_len" "void **reply" "size_t *reply_len" "size_t max_reply" "int64_t timeout_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_osproc_serve "int ctrl_fd" "xtc_osproc_handler_fn handler" "void *arg"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_osproc_try_wait "xtc_osproc_t *p" "int *status"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_osproc_wait "xtc_osproc_t *p" "int *status" "int64_t timeout_ns"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_osproc_destroy "xtc_osproc_t *p"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions run work in a separate operating-system process -- the
.Dq default-to-fork
tier -- as opposed to
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
which spawns an in-process fiber.
Use this tier for un-cooperative or untrusted work that must not run in
a loop thread, and for one-process-per-connection isolation.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_osproc_spawn
forks a child described by
.Fa opts .
Exactly one of
.Va opts->argv
(the child
.Xr execvp 3 Ns s
a program) or
.Va opts->fn
(the child runs
.Fn fn ctrl_fd arg
and
.Xr _exit 2 Ns s
with its return value) must be set.
When
.Va opts->ctrl_socket
is non-zero an
.Dv AF_UNIX
.Dv SOCK_STREAM
socketpair is created; the parent end is returned by
.Fn xtc_osproc_ctrl_fd
(it is
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
and
.Dv O_CLOEXEC ,
pollable with
.Xr xtc_proc 3
or wrappable with
.Xr xtc_net 3 ) ,
and the child end is delivered to a forked callback as its
.Fa ctrl_fd
argument or, for an exec'd program, in the environment variable
.Ev XTC_CTRL_FD .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_osproc_signal
sends
.Fa sig
to the child.
.Fn xtc_osproc_try_wait
reaps the child without blocking, returning
.Dv XTC_OK
and the raw
.Xr waitpid 2
status if it has exited or
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
if it is still running.
.Fn xtc_osproc_wait
parks the calling fiber until the child exits (or
.Fa timeout_ns
elapses; a negative value waits forever), then reaps it; it never
blocks the loop thread.
.Fn xtc_osproc_destroy
closes the control socket, releases the handle, and best-effort reaps
an already-exited child; it does not kill a still-running child.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_osproc_isolated_spawn
spawns a worker running
.Fa fn
over a control socket, the setup for the request/reply protocol.
The parent then ships a framed request with
.Fn xtc_osproc_call
.Pq which parks for the reply, capped at Fa max_reply No bytes, up to Fa timeout_ns ,
while the child loops in
.Fn xtc_osproc_serve
handling framed requests with its
.Fa handler
until the control socket closes.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
On Linux (>= 5.3) a
.Xr pidfd 2
backs
.Fn xtc_osproc_wait
so the caller parks on a readable descriptor with no process-wide
.Dv SIGCHLD
handler; elsewhere
.Fn xtc_osproc_wait
falls back to a cooperative
.Xr waitpid 2
.Dv WNOHANG
poll that yields between probes.
The library never installs a
.Dv SIGCHLD
handler, so it does not collide with an embedder's signal handling.
.Pp
An xtc executor is multi-threaded;
.Xr fork 2
duplicates only the calling thread.
The exec path is therefore safe.
A
.Va fn
(fork-only) callback runs in a half-initialised child and must
restrict itself to async-signal-safe work until it re-initialises its
own runtime.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_osproc_spawn
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
on success, or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL ,
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL .
The wait functions return
.Dv XTC_OK
(exited),
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
(running or timed out), or a negative error.
On a platform without
.Xr fork 2
(Windows) every function returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_net 3 ,
.Xr xtc_sync 3
